status: "published"
title: "Feedback Items with Voting and Filtering"
label: "Feedback Items"
order: 4
description: "Build feedback items with a voting system, status workflow, and filtering using Drizzle ORM relations, PostgreSQL enums, and URL-based query parameters."
---

With boards in place, it's time to build the feature that makes TeamPulse useful: **feedback items**. 

Users will submit bugs, feature requests, and ideas, then vote on what matters most. Your team triages items through a status workflow - from "New" to "Done" - giving everyone visibility into what's being worked on.

This module introduces several new patterns: **parent-child relationships** in Drizzle, **PostgreSQL enums** for type-safe values, **vote toggling with transactions**, and **URL-based filtering** that works with the browser's back button.

**Time:** ~3-4 hours

**What you'll accomplish:**
- Design feedback items and votes schemas with proper relationships
- Build CRUD operations with multi-tenant security
- Implement voting with toggle logic and atomic transactions
- Create a status workflow (New → Planned → In Progress → Done)
- Add filtering and sorting via URL search parameters

**Technologies used:**
- [Drizzle ORM](https://orm.drizzle.team) - Database queries with relations
- [next-safe-action](https://next-safe-action.dev) - Type-safe server actions
- [React Hook Form](https://react-hook-form.com) - Form state management
- [Zod](https://zod.dev) - Schema validation

**Prerequisites:** Complete [Module 3: Data Model & First Feature](./data-model-and-first-feature) first. You need the boards feature working.

---

## What We're Building

Feedback items are the heart of TeamPulse. Each item belongs to a board and tracks a bug report, feature request, or idea. Users vote on items to surface what matters most, and the team moves items through status stages to show progress.

The `feedback_vote` table creates a many-to-many relationship between users and feedback items. We also store `voteCount` directly on the feedback item - this denormalization avoids counting votes on every page load, improving performance significantly for boards with many items.

```
feedback_item                    feedback_vote
├── id                           ├── id
├── boardId (FK)                 ├── feedbackItemId (FK)
├── authorId (FK)                ├── userId (FK)
├── title                        ├── createdAt
├── description
├── type (bug/feature/idea)
├── status (new/planned/in_progress/done/closed)
├── voteCount
├── createdAt
├── updatedAt
```

---

## Step 1: Design the Schema

This schema introduces PostgreSQL enums via `pgEnum`. Unlike string columns, enums are validated at the database level - you can't insert an invalid value. Drizzle generates TypeScript types from these enums, giving you type safety from database to UI.

NB: if you are using other databases, such as MySQL or SqLite, the same may not apply. Please refer to the documentation for the specific database you are using to understand how to implement the same patterns.

### Create the Feedback Items Table

The feedback items table references both `boards` (which board it belongs to) and `users` (who authored it). We add indexes on the columns we'll filter and sort by: `status`, `type`, and `voteCount`.

```typescript {% title="packages/database/src/schema/feedback-item.ts" %}
import { relations } from 'drizzle-orm';
import {
  index,
  integer,
  pgEnum,
  pgTable,
  text,
  timestamp,
  unique,
} from 'drizzle-orm/pg-core';

import { board } from './boards';
import { user } from './core';

// Enums for type-safe status and type values
export const feedbackTypeEnum = pgEnum('feedback_type', [
  'bug',
  'feature',
  'idea',
]);

export const feedbackStatusEnum = pgEnum('feedback_status', [
  'new',
  'planned',
  'in_progress',
  'done',
  'closed',
]);

export const feedbackItem = pgTable(
  'feedback_item',
  {
    id: text('id').primaryKey(),
    boardId: text('board_id')
      .notNull()
      .references(() => board.id, { onDelete: 'cascade' }),
    authorId: text('author_id')
      .references(() => user.id, { onDelete: 'cascade' }),
    title: text('title').notNull(),
    description: text('description'),
    type: feedbackTypeEnum('type').notNull().default('idea'),
    status: feedbackStatusEnum('status').notNull().default('new'),
    voteCount: integer('vote_count').notNull().default(0),
    createdAt: timestamp('created_at').notNull().defaultNow(),
    updatedAt: timestamp('updated_at')
      .notNull()
      .defaultNow()
      .$onUpdate(() => new Date()),
  },
  (table) => [
    index('feedback_item_board_id_idx').on(table.boardId),
    index('feedback_item_author_id_idx').on(table.authorId),
    index('feedback_item_status_idx').on(table.status),
    index('feedback_item_type_idx').on(table.type),
    index('feedback_item_vote_count_idx').on(table.voteCount),
  ],
);

export const feedbackItemRelations = relations(
  feedbackItem,
  ({ one, many }) => ({
    board: one(board, {
      fields: [feedbackItem.boardId],
      references: [board.id],
    }),
    author: one(user, {
      fields: [feedbackItem.authorId],
      references: [user.id],
    }),
    votes: many(feedbackVote),
  }),
);
```

Don't worry if you see errors in the code editor. We'll fix them in the next steps as we add the remaining schema definitions.

### Create the Votes Table

The votes table tracks which users voted on which feedback items. The `unique` constraint on `(feedbackItemId, userId)` prevents double-voting at the database level - even if a race condition occurs in the application code, the database will reject duplicate votes.

```typescript {% title="packages/database/src/schema/feedback-item.ts" %}
export const feedbackVote = pgTable(
  'feedback_vote',
  {
    id: text('id').primaryKey(),
    feedbackItemId: text('feedback_item_id')
      .notNull()
      .references(() => feedbackItem.id, { onDelete: 'cascade' }),
    userId: text('user_id')
      .notNull()
      .references(() => user.id, { onDelete: 'cascade' }),
    createdAt: timestamp('created_at').notNull().defaultNow(),
  },
  (table) => [
    // Prevent double-voting
    unique('feedback_vote_unique').on(table.feedbackItemId, table.userId),
    index('feedback_vote_feedback_item_id_idx').on(table.feedbackItemId),
    index('feedback_vote_user_id_idx').on(table.userId),
  ],
);

export const feedbackVoteRelations = relations(feedbackVote, ({ one }) => ({
  feedbackItem: one(feedbackItem, {
    fields: [feedbackVote.feedbackItemId],
    references: [feedbackItem.id],
  }),
  user: one(user, {
    fields: [feedbackVote.userId],
    references: [user.id],
  }),
}));
```

### Export the Schema

Add the new schema file to the barrel export so it's available throughout the application:

```typescript {% title="packages/database/src/schema/schema.ts" %}
// Add this export alongside the existing ones
export * from './feedback-item';
```

### Run the Migration

Generate and apply the migration. This will create both the `feedback_item` and `feedback_vote` tables, plus the PostgreSQL enum types:

```bash
# Generate migration
pnpm --filter @kit/database drizzle:generate
```

If the command is successful, you should see the following output:

```
